1
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:07,540
The parcel was lying on the doormat when I came up the stairs at ten past six,

2
00:00:07,540 --> 00:00:11,280
squared to the edge of the coir as though someone had measured it.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,175
Brown paper, a great deal of string,

4
00:00:14,175 --> 00:00:17,620
and my name across the front in a hand I could not place.

5
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:22,770
It was not a clerk's hand, nor Mrs Pettigrew's round capitals from the rent

6
00:00:22,770 --> 00:00:23,370
receipts.

7
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:25,420
The letters leaned backwards.

8
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:30,440
The M of Millicent had been made in two strokes, like a pair of tents.

9
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:33,740
"You've had a visitor," said a voice below me.

10
00:00:34,460 --> 00:00:38,800
Marjorie Pettigrew stood on the half-landing with her keys in her fist, looking up.

11
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:40,800
"I heard the door go at four.

12
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:43,300
I looked out, and there was nobody on the stairs."

13
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:44,920
"The post, perhaps."

14
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:47,660
"The post doesn't come up in carpet slippers.

15
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:50,560
She said it flatly, and went down again.

16
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,520
There was no stamp and no postmark.

17
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:57,100
I carried the thing inside, set it on the kitchen table,

18
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:00,800
and cut the string with the bread knife before I had taken off my hat.

19
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:06,400
Under the paper was a sheet of newsprint, folded in four and soft as cloth.

20
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:08,800
Under that was a television.

21
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:12,141
It was small, no bigger than a bread bin,

22
00:01:12,141 --> 00:01:16,960
in a walnut case with a cloth grille at the side and two brass knobs gone dull.

23
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:20,000
The glass was grey and slightly convex.

24
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:25,720
I had seen such sets in photographs of the nineteen-fifties, and never one so clean.

25
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:28,620
There was no dust in the grooves of the knobs.

26
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:33,007
A flex lay coiled beside it, tied with a twist of thread,

27
00:01:33,007 --> 00:01:35,400
ending in a plug with two round pins.

28
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:36,960
I turned it over.

29
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:41,500
There was no maker's plate, no number, no label of any kind.

30
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:47,236
I read what I could of the newsprint by the kitchen lamp, a column of cattle prices,

31
00:01:47,236 --> 00:01:51,900
an advertisement for a corset, and then I stopped, because I was very tired.

32
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:53,680
I folded it again.

33
00:01:54,360 --> 00:01:57,425
I put the television on the sideboard in the front room,

34
00:01:57,425 --> 00:02:00,380
with the flex hanging loose, and I did not plug it in.

35
00:02:00,820 --> 00:02:02,040
I would like that to be understood.

36
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:05,200
I ate bread and cheese standing at the window.

37
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:09,354
At half past nine I went round the flat putting out the lamps,

38
00:02:09,354 --> 00:02:11,920
until only the one on my bedside table remained.

39
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:13,720
I wound my watch.

40
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:15,040
I put out the lamp.

41
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:17,960
The dark in that flat is a complete one.

42
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:22,817
The shutters fit well, and the window gives on to the blind wall of the chandler's

43
00:02:22,817 --> 00:02:23,417
yard.

44
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:25,940
For some seconds I heard only the pipes.

45
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:31,723
Then a faint hiss came through the open door, steady as a kettle a long way off,

46
00:02:31,723 --> 00:02:34,800
and a dull light began to move across the ceiling.

47
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:37,100
I went through in my nightgown.

48
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:40,440
The flex still hung over the edge of the sideboard,

49
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:42,700
its plug swinging a little above the floor.

50
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:44,340
The glass was lit.

51
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:48,447
It showed a street in a grey without any colour at all, wet,

52
00:02:48,447 --> 00:02:50,500
with a gutter down the middle.

53
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:55,840
Terraced houses leaned in on both sides, and I knew the shape of the chimney pots.

54
00:02:56,640 --> 00:03:00,272
A woman in a long green coat, the only colour on the screen,

55
00:03:00,272 --> 00:03:03,360
was walking away from me towards a door at the end.

56
00:03:03,980 --> 00:03:04,880
I knew the door.

57
00:03:05,380 --> 00:03:06,680
I could not think from where.

58
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:12,022
She put her hand to the knocker, and stopped, and began, very slowly,

59
00:03:12,022 --> 00:03:13,220
to turn her head.

60
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:16,120
She did not turn far.

61
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:19,687
I saw the line of her cheek, grey as the street,

62
00:03:19,687 --> 00:03:24,200
and then the picture dissolved into white lines, and the glass went black.

63
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:27,520
I stood there until my feet were cold.

64
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:30,414
Then I lay on the bed with my eyes open,

65
00:03:30,414 --> 00:03:33,040
and did not once put out my hand to the lamp.

66
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:38,640
In the morning I examined the thing as I would any item brought to me for listing.

67
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:41,440
The veneer was lifting at one corner.

68
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,553
The back panel was held by four slotted screws,

69
00:03:44,553 --> 00:03:46,780
and I took them out with a kitchen knife.

70
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:51,780
Inside were valves, a cracked condenser, and a good deal of dust.

71
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:54,120
No cable ran to anything.

72
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:58,953
The flex ended in a plug, but its other end ended in nothing at all,

73
00:03:58,953 --> 00:04:02,080
merely cut and bound with black tape gone brittle.

74
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:05,980
I put the screws in a saucer and drew back the shutters.

75
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:11,420
The set sat on the sideboard, a dead brown box, and reflected the window.

76
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:16,620
That night I tried it again, and the night after, with variations.

77
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:18,760
I covered the keyhole.

78
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:21,420
I stopped the gap under the door with a towel.

79
00:04:21,980 --> 00:04:24,157
I left a candle burning in the passage,

80
00:04:24,157 --> 00:04:27,060
and the glass stayed blank until I pinched the wick.

81
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:32,700
Then it came on, and the street was the same street, and the woman was further on.

82
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:35,400
On the second night she knocked.

83
00:04:36,120 --> 00:04:39,344
On the third, the door opened inwards on a dark hall,

84
00:04:39,344 --> 00:04:41,960
and she stood a long while looking into it.

85
00:04:42,840 --> 00:04:46,040
Each night gave me perhaps four minutes more than the last.

86
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:50,200
I timed it by my watch, holding the watch an inch from my face.

87
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:53,460
On the fourth morning I knew the street.

88
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:57,649
I was buying a loaf at Dunmore's, and I saw, across the road,

89
00:04:57,649 --> 00:04:59,120
the leaning chimney pots.

90
00:04:59,780 --> 00:05:00,680
Candlewick Lane.

91
00:05:01,680 --> 00:05:05,160
I walked down it with the loaf under my arm and counted my paces.

92
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:10,520
It took four minutes, at an ordinary gait, from my own door to the door at the end.

93
00:05:11,180 --> 00:05:11,780
It was shut.

94
00:05:12,440 --> 00:05:14,700
It had a brass knocker in the shape of a hand.

95
00:05:15,180 --> 00:05:16,400
I did not touch it.

96
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:21,638
Marjorie Pettigrew has kept the keys to St Ninian's for thirty years,

97
00:05:21,638 --> 00:05:26,388
and has the second-floor front, and gives tea on Thursdays whether she is asked or

98
00:05:26,388 --> 00:05:26,940
not.

99
00:05:26,940 --> 00:05:28,220
I went down that afternoon.

100
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:31,480
Her room smelled of coal and lavender water.

101
00:05:32,120 --> 00:05:35,980
She poured with a steady hand and told me about the damp in the cellar.

102
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:38,020
"Who had my flat before me?

103
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:38,540
I said.

104
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:39,980
"Oh, a woman.

105
00:05:40,780 --> 00:05:41,340
Quiet.

106
00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:42,560
She stirred her cup.

107
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:44,660
"Kept very odd hours.

108
00:05:45,320 --> 00:05:46,860
Burned no lights at all, you know.

109
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:51,320
I used to look up from the yard and the windows were always black.

110
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:55,540
I said to her once, you'll ruin your eyes, and she said she saw better."

111
00:05:56,140 --> 00:05:57,580
"Might I look at the tenancy book?

112
00:05:58,040 --> 00:05:58,920
Only for the dates.

113
00:05:59,240 --> 00:05:59,940
It is my trade."

114
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:02,000
Marjorie put down her spoon.

115
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:05,120
The clock on her mantel was loud in the pause.

116
00:06:05,860 --> 00:06:09,624
She looked, not at me, but at the cupboard door on the landing,

117
00:06:09,624 --> 00:06:12,254
which stood open a hand's breadth behind me;

118
00:06:12,254 --> 00:06:15,540
I knew this because I saw her eyes go past my shoulder.

119
00:06:15,540 --> 00:06:19,120
"The pages for that flat were taken out," she said.

120
00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:20,500
"A long time ago.

121
00:06:21,140 --> 00:06:22,380
I couldn't tell you by whom."

122
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:24,000
"But you keep the book."

123
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:28,960
"I keep what's left of it," said Marjorie Pettigrew, and poured me more tea,

124
00:06:28,960 --> 00:06:30,280
though my cup was full.

125
00:06:31,700 --> 00:06:36,260
I did not sleep that night, or not in the way I understood sleep.

126
00:06:37,180 --> 00:06:41,514
The set came on at ten to ten, as it had on every night since the first,

127
00:06:41,514 --> 00:06:43,320
the moment I put out the lamp.

128
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:46,180
I lay with my face turned to it.

129
00:06:46,940 --> 00:06:51,200
The woman in the green coat came through the street door of St Ninian's.

130
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:55,960
I knew the door by its cracked fanlight, and the boot-scraper bent at one end.

131
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:58,140
She did not look about her.

132
00:06:58,660 --> 00:06:59,260
She climbed.

133
00:07:00,080 --> 00:07:01,860
The camera did not follow her.

134
00:07:02,420 --> 00:07:05,936
It hung fixed in the corner above the third-floor landing,

135
00:07:05,936 --> 00:07:11,028
and it received her from above, so that I saw the parting of her hair and the top of

136
00:07:11,028 --> 00:07:12,240
her hat and no face.

137
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:14,520
She stopped at my door.

138
00:07:15,140 --> 00:07:16,800
It opened for her without a key.

139
00:07:17,580 --> 00:07:22,235
Then the picture changed to the inside of my flat, from the same high corner,

140
00:07:22,235 --> 00:07:25,620
which I measured against my own ceiling and found exact.

141
00:07:26,380 --> 00:07:27,080
The fireplace.

142
00:07:27,820 --> 00:07:30,700
The bookcase with its two shelves of parish registers.

143
00:07:31,460 --> 00:07:33,560
The window, and the yard beyond it.

144
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:39,583
In the middle of the floor, where I have only ever kept a card-table folded against

145
00:07:39,583 --> 00:07:43,920
the wall, stood a round table of dark oak on a single carved pedestal.

146
00:07:44,640 --> 00:07:47,140
She laid upon it a white cup and saucer.

147
00:07:47,660 --> 00:07:52,460
She laid beside them a lamp with a green glass shade, and did not light it.

148
00:07:53,060 --> 00:07:57,080
She sat down in front of both and put her hands flat on the wood.

149
00:07:58,020 --> 00:08:01,560
I am a cataloguer, and I did what I am trained to do.

150
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:06,764
I wrote down what I had seen, in pencil, on the back of a Diocesan circular,

151
00:08:06,764 --> 00:08:07,620
with the time.

152
00:08:08,540 --> 00:08:09,140
Oak.

153
00:08:09,380 --> 00:08:10,020
Pedestal.

154
00:08:10,580 --> 00:08:11,520
Four claw feet.

155
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:12,860
Green shade.

156
00:08:13,780 --> 00:08:17,700
Then I watched her sit until the screen went out at ten to one.

157
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:21,996
At nine the next morning Marjorie knocked, carrying a tray,

158
00:08:21,996 --> 00:08:25,660
since I had missed her Thursday habit and she meant to correct it.

159
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:30,951
She found me at the desk with the telephone beside me and a furnisher's catalogue

160
00:08:30,951 --> 00:08:32,820
from Dunbar Road open at my elbow.

161
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:36,000
I do not remember sending for the catalogue.

162
00:08:36,420 --> 00:08:39,462
I remember the page, and my finger on it,

163
00:08:39,462 --> 00:08:45,693
and the voice of a young man reading back my order: oak, round, pedestal, four feet,

164
00:08:45,693 --> 00:08:46,880
delivery Friday.

165
00:08:47,640 --> 00:08:48,780
"And the lamp, madam?

166
00:08:49,400 --> 00:08:50,960
I had said that I had a lamp.

167
00:08:51,620 --> 00:08:54,420
"Oak," said Marjorie, reading over my shoulder.

168
00:08:55,180 --> 00:08:56,660
Her tray did not tremble.

169
00:08:57,220 --> 00:08:57,820
"Round."

170
00:08:58,240 --> 00:08:59,760
"You needn't say it like that."

171
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:01,640
"I'm saying it how it is.

172
00:09:02,240 --> 00:09:05,620
She set the tray down and looked at the card-table against the wall.

173
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:07,860
"I'd want it measured, if I were you.

174
00:09:08,280 --> 00:09:08,960
Before it comes."

175
00:09:09,780 --> 00:09:13,000
I rang the newspaper archive at Queen's Wharf that afternoon.

176
00:09:13,580 --> 00:09:16,430
The wrapper I had kept flat between two boards;

177
00:09:16,430 --> 00:09:20,432
I read them the masthead and the date, and a notice, third column,

178
00:09:20,432 --> 00:09:23,100
which I had not troubled to read until then.

179
00:09:23,640 --> 00:09:25,585
I was put through to a Miss Drummond,

180
00:09:25,585 --> 00:09:29,687
who said she was the oldest clerk they had and the only one who still knew the

181
00:09:29,687 --> 00:09:30,287
shelving.

182
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:32,460
She was gone eleven minutes.

183
00:09:33,140 --> 00:09:35,440
When she came back she was breathing hard.

184
00:09:36,020 --> 00:09:37,880
"I have the volume open, Miss Holloway.

185
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:39,280
The notice is there.

186
00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:40,620
'Miss M.

187
00:09:40,620 --> 00:09:43,440
Holloway, cataloguer, St Ninian's.

188
00:09:43,440 --> 00:09:44,600
Interred on the fourth.

189
00:09:45,240 --> 00:09:46,140
That is the wording."

190
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:48,480
"Then it is a coincidence of names."

191
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:51,620
"The address is not a coincidence, my dear.

192
00:09:52,320 --> 00:09:53,780
Paper moved on her end.

193
00:09:54,440 --> 00:09:56,900
"This number is dated the fifteenth.

194
00:09:56,900 --> 00:09:58,620
You say the burial was on the fourth.

195
00:09:58,620 --> 00:10:00,160
Eleven days.

196
00:10:00,840 --> 00:10:05,196
They printed her funeral after she was in the ground, and the notice was placed,

197
00:10:05,196 --> 00:10:07,700
it says here, by instruction of the deceased."

198
00:10:09,220 --> 00:10:11,540
I did not mean to sleep that night.

199
00:10:12,260 --> 00:10:16,972
On the ninth evening I carried the lamp from the writing desk to the new oak table,

200
00:10:16,972 --> 00:10:19,300
which had come on the Friday as promised,

201
00:10:19,300 --> 00:10:22,820
and set it down beside the cup I had put out without thinking.

202
00:10:23,620 --> 00:10:24,720
Then I sat.

203
00:10:25,680 --> 00:10:30,640
I had made up my mind, if that is the phrase, on the strength of nothing but temper.

204
00:10:31,480 --> 00:10:34,720
If the set lit only in darkness, I would give it none.

205
00:10:35,620 --> 00:10:39,680
The lamp was a brass one with a green shade and a forty-watt bulb.

206
00:10:40,240 --> 00:10:41,760
I had owned it eleven years.

207
00:10:42,760 --> 00:10:47,649
By ten o'clock I had read the same page of a Kirkcudbright burial register four

208
00:10:47,649 --> 00:10:48,249
times.

209
00:10:48,760 --> 00:10:52,894
By midnight I had stopped reading and was watching the walnut case,

210
00:10:52,894 --> 00:10:55,300
its blind grey face turned to the room.

211
00:10:56,220 --> 00:10:58,900
The radiator knocked twice and went quiet.

212
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:02,335
Somewhere below, a door closed in the stairwell,

213
00:11:02,335 --> 00:11:05,520
and I counted the steps until I could no longer hear them.

214
00:11:06,460 --> 00:11:08,880
At twenty minutes to two the screen woke.

215
00:11:09,620 --> 00:11:10,360
There was no click.

216
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:15,850
The glass went from grey to white and then to the street, Candlewick Lane, wet,

217
00:11:15,850 --> 00:11:20,640
with a gas lamp at the corner, though there has been no gas lamp there since the

218
00:11:20,640 --> 00:11:21,240
war.

219
00:11:21,700 --> 00:11:23,780
My own lamp burned on beside it.

220
00:11:24,340 --> 00:11:28,190
I put my hand flat on the table and felt the grain of the oak,

221
00:11:28,190 --> 00:11:29,680
and was grateful for it.

222
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:34,151
The picture moved indoors, to the stairs, then to my door,

223
00:11:34,151 --> 00:11:36,220
then to the room in which I sat.

224
00:11:37,080 --> 00:11:41,398
The fixed eye near the ceiling looked down upon a table, a cup,

225
00:11:41,398 --> 00:11:43,180
a lamp with a green shade.

226
00:11:44,020 --> 00:11:47,640
A woman sat at it in a green coat, her back half to me.

227
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:51,120
She had been sitting, I understood, for some while.

228
00:11:51,940 --> 00:11:52,960
She turned her head.

229
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:58,994
She looked into the lens, and so at me, and her face was mine, only rested,

230
00:11:58,994 --> 00:12:01,440
and without any fear in it at all.

231
00:12:02,180 --> 00:12:06,020
She lifted a slip of paper in two fingers and held it to the glass.

232
00:12:06,920 --> 00:12:08,220
I rose and went close.

233
00:12:09,120 --> 00:12:12,060
It was a page torn from a ledger, ruled in blue.

234
00:12:12,880 --> 00:12:15,020
Across the top was written tomorrow's date.

235
00:12:15,020 --> 00:12:21,043
Below it, a line of tenancy in a hand I knew from thirty Christmas cards: Marjorie

236
00:12:21,043 --> 00:12:23,100
Pettigrew, for the landlord.

237
00:12:23,420 --> 00:12:27,928
And then, where the tenant's name should be, mine, not yet signed,

238
00:12:27,928 --> 00:12:29,840
the pen waiting on the line.

239
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:33,220
I was on the landing before I had put on my shoes.

240
00:12:34,200 --> 00:12:37,669
Marjorie opened at the third knock, in a quilted dressing gown,

241
00:12:37,669 --> 00:12:42,020
with her keys already in her hand, as if she had been standing behind the door.

242
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:47,520
She looked at my face, and was not at all surprised, and stepped back to let me in.

243
00:12:48,140 --> 00:12:49,440
I asked her nothing.

244
00:12:50,060 --> 00:12:52,260
I said the date, and the word lease.

245
00:12:53,100 --> 00:12:55,540
"Three times," said Marjorie Pettigrew.

246
00:12:56,180 --> 00:12:57,700
Her voice was perfectly level.

247
00:12:58,320 --> 00:13:01,000
"I have signed your name three times, Millicent.

248
00:13:01,660 --> 00:13:06,033
Each time for a woman who went out in a green coat one evening and was not seen

249
00:13:06,033 --> 00:13:06,633
again."

250
00:13:07,180 --> 00:13:07,920
"You knew.

251
00:13:08,540 --> 00:13:10,540
You poured my tea and you knew."

252
00:13:11,300 --> 00:13:12,960
"I knew what I was paid to know.

253
00:13:13,540 --> 00:13:15,960
I've had thirty years to learn what I wasn't.

254
00:13:16,660 --> 00:13:18,800
She turned the keys over once in her palm.

255
00:13:19,580 --> 00:13:20,520
"And I liked you.

256
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:22,640
That's the part I can't mend."

257
00:13:23,460 --> 00:13:24,220
"And the television?"

258
00:13:24,920 --> 00:13:28,180
"It always comes back to the cupboard on the landing," she said.

259
00:13:28,660 --> 00:13:30,680
"I have never once had to put it there."

260
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:33,940
I carried the set down myself.

261
00:13:35,060 --> 00:13:38,100
Marjorie did not offer to help, and I did not ask her.

262
00:13:38,900 --> 00:13:43,460
It was heavier than I remembered, or I was lighter; I could not tell which.

263
00:13:44,380 --> 00:13:48,455
I had wound up the flex in the afternoon, though it had never needed a plug,

264
00:13:48,455 --> 00:13:50,975
and wrapped the whole in the brittle newsprint,

265
00:13:50,975 --> 00:13:53,120
folding the corners as I had found them.

266
00:13:53,920 --> 00:13:56,260
The paper left a grey dust on my cuffs.

267
00:13:57,060 --> 00:14:01,902
The cupboard on the landing was narrower than a wardrobe and smelled of camphor and

268
00:14:01,902 --> 00:14:02,660
cold plaster.

269
00:14:03,660 --> 00:14:07,760
Marjorie unlocked it with the third key on her ring, without looking for it.

270
00:14:08,560 --> 00:14:13,319
Inside were two shelves of parish ledgers, a tin of floor wax, and,

271
00:14:13,319 --> 00:14:17,580
on the upper shelf, side by side, two parcels in grey paper.

272
00:14:18,440 --> 00:14:20,640
They were the same size as mine.

273
00:14:20,640 --> 00:14:23,912
The newsprint on each had gone the colour of weak tea,

274
00:14:23,912 --> 00:14:28,760
and the string was knotted in the manner I use myself, a double turn and a slip.

275
00:14:29,520 --> 00:14:30,720
I did not touch them.

276
00:14:31,220 --> 00:14:35,073
I set my parcel on the shelf beside them, squared it to the edge,

277
00:14:35,073 --> 00:14:39,164
and found that I had left a space of exactly two inches between each,

278
00:14:39,164 --> 00:14:41,120
as one does when arranging books.

279
00:14:41,860 --> 00:14:44,160
"Shut it," said Marjorie from her doorway.

280
00:14:44,580 --> 00:14:46,080
"Don't stand about with it open."

281
00:14:47,220 --> 00:14:47,820
"Marjorie.

282
00:14:48,300 --> 00:14:51,080
If I go now, tonight, and take a train—"

283
00:14:51,580 --> 00:14:53,040
"Then you'll be the one who went.

284
00:14:53,620 --> 00:14:55,200
They all went.

285
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:56,680
She did not raise her voice.

286
00:14:57,380 --> 00:14:58,500
"Shut it, Millicent."

287
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:00,180
I shut it.

288
00:15:00,840 --> 00:15:04,620
The latch was stiff and made a small dry sound like a knuckle.

289
00:15:05,320 --> 00:15:09,820
She locked it, put the keys into her pocket, and went in without another word.

290
00:15:09,820 --> 00:15:12,020
I heard her draw the bolt.

291
00:15:13,020 --> 00:15:17,940
That morning, before any of this, I had walked to Candlewick Lane and bought a coat.

292
00:15:18,860 --> 00:15:19,620
I say bought.

293
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:23,831
I remember the shop, a narrow outfitter's with a brass bell,

294
00:15:23,831 --> 00:15:27,400
and I remember counting out four pound notes and some silver.

295
00:15:28,080 --> 00:15:30,740
I do not remember choosing the colour.

296
00:15:30,740 --> 00:15:35,992
It was green, a dull bottle green, and it fitted across the shoulders as if cut from

297
00:15:35,992 --> 00:15:36,680
my measure.

298
00:15:36,680 --> 00:15:40,060
The assistant said it had been in the window some while.

299
00:15:40,840 --> 00:15:42,440
At six I put it on.

300
00:15:43,160 --> 00:15:44,180
I had not meant to.

301
00:15:44,680 --> 00:15:47,078
I stood in the kitchen with the lamp lit,

302
00:15:47,078 --> 00:15:50,880
intending to wait for morning and the agent's office and a train,

303
00:15:50,880 --> 00:15:55,500
and found my arms already in the sleeves and the buttons done up to the throat.

304
00:15:56,240 --> 00:15:59,677
I went out onto the landing and down to the half-landing,

305
00:15:59,677 --> 00:16:04,320
where the stairwell turns and the rail is worn pale by thirty years of hands.

306
00:16:05,080 --> 00:16:05,980
I stopped there.

307
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:08,560
I put my right hand on the rail.

308
00:16:09,440 --> 00:16:13,886
The timer light on the floor below clicked off, as it does after two minutes,

309
00:16:13,886 --> 00:16:15,560
and the whole well went dark.

310
00:16:16,520 --> 00:16:17,800
Then I heard it.

311
00:16:18,700 --> 00:16:25,217
Not music, nor voices; only the faint high note a set makes when it warms, once,

312
00:16:25,217 --> 00:16:29,860
twice, a third time, each slightly apart from the others.

313
00:16:30,940 --> 00:16:35,717
A seam of light appeared under the cupboard door, and widened, and was grey,

314
00:16:35,717 --> 00:16:36,660
and was steady.

315
00:16:37,400 --> 00:16:38,900
I could see my shoes in it.

316
00:16:39,560 --> 00:16:41,300
I did not need to go back and look.

317
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:46,091
I knew what the three small screens were showing, because I could feel,

318
00:16:46,091 --> 00:16:50,320
between my shoulder blades, the exact place where each lens was resting.

319
00:16:51,180 --> 00:16:56,420
I took my hand from the rail, and began, one step at a time, to go down.

